ML_PAGE("l1-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 1 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 1 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 33 multiple-choice questions and 5 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>33</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>5</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l1\" data-quiz=\"1\">Start Lecture 1 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"l1p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">13 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - CORE CONCEPTS &amp; TRAPS</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know cold\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s2\" title=\"1. What you should know cold\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Key facts table</button></li><li data-full=\"2. The biggest MCQ traps\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s3\" title=\"2. The biggest MCQ traps\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Common traps</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - PRACTICE SET 1</li><li data-full=\"3. MCQ Practice - Set 1\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s5\" title=\"3. MCQ Practice - Set 1\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Practice set 1 (Q1-25)</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Answers - Set 1\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s6\" title=\"4. Answers - Set 1\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Answer key: set 1</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - CODING QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"5. Coding Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s8\" title=\"5. Coding Questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Coding: classification or regression</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Coding Q2 - What does `.fit()` conceptually do?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s9\" title=\"6. Coding Q2 - What does .fit() conceptually do?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Coding: what fit() does</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Coding Q3 - Spot the overfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s10\" title=\"7. Coding Q3 - Spot the overfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Coding: spot overfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Coding Q4 - Instance-based or model-based?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s11\" title=\"8. Coding Q4 - Instance-based or model-based?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Coding: instance or model-based</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Coding Q5 - Batch vs online\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s12\" title=\"9. Coding Q5 - Batch vs online\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Coding: batch vs online</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - HARDER SCENARIOS</li><li data-full=\"10. Harder MCQ Set - the kinds that actually catch people\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s14\" title=\"10. Harder MCQ Set - the kinds that actually catch people\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Harder scenarios (Q26-30)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART E - FRAMEWORKS</li><li data-full=\"11. Framework questions are mostly about emphasis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s16\" title=\"11. Framework questions are mostly about emphasis\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Framework emphasis</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Framework MCQ traps\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s17\" title=\"12. Framework MCQ traps\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Framework traps (Q31-33)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART F - FINAL REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"13. A final rapid-fire test\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1p-s19\" title=\"13. A final rapid-fire test\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Rapid-fire review</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 1 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>13 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>For <strong>Lecture 1</strong>, I'd prepare you mainly for <strong>conceptual MCQs with scenario traps</strong>, because the lecture is much more about identifying ML types, learning frameworks, data problems, and overfitting/underfitting than writing code.</p>\n<p>The lecture's own summary says the core examinable areas are: the general and engineering-oriented definition of ML, supervised/unsupervised/reinforcement learning, batch/online and model/instance-based learning, and the KDD, CRISP-DM, and SEMMA frameworks.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d1\">PART A - CORE CONCEPTS &amp; TRAPS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know cold</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>What you must recognise instantly</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine Learning</td>\n<td>Learns from data rather than only explicit rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>Task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>Experience/data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>Performance measure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classification</td>\n<td>Predict a category</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regression</td>\n<td>Predict a numerical value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clustering</td>\n<td>Discover groups without labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anomaly detection</td>\n<td>Find unusual observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervised</td>\n<td>Labelled data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unsupervised</td>\n<td>Unlabelled data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforcement</td>\n<td>Actions + reward/punishment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Batch learning</td>\n<td>Train on available data, deploy, retrain later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Online learning</td>\n<td>Update incrementally as data arrives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Out-of-core</td>\n<td>Process dataset piece by piece because it is too large for memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instance-based</td>\n<td>Compare new observation with similar stored examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model-based</td>\n<td>Learn a general relationship/model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overfitting</td>\n<td>Great on training, bad on unseen data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Underfitting</td>\n<td>Too simple; bad even on training data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature engineering</td>\n<td>Create/select more useful representations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KDD</td>\n<td>Data → knowledge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CRISP-DM</td>\n<td>Business problem → deployment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SEMMA</td>\n<td>Sample → Explore → Modify → Model → Assess</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture explicitly classifies ML in three different ways: by supervision, by incremental-learning ability, and by how it generalizes.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. The biggest MCQ traps</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h5>Trap: classification vs regression</h5>\n<p>If the output is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Fraud / Not Fraud\n</code></pre>\n<p>that's <strong>classification</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the output is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">RM550,000\n</code></pre>\n<p>that's <strong>regression</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines classification as assigning categories and regression as predicting real numerical values.</p>\n<h5>Trap: classification vs clustering</h5>\n<p>If the categories are already known:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Premium\nStandard\nBudget\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you're predicting one → <strong>classification</strong>.</p>\n<p>If no groups were given and the algorithm discovers them → <strong>clustering</strong>.</p>\n<h5>Trap: batch vs online</h5>\n<p>The words \"real-time prediction\" do <strong>not automatically mean online learning</strong>.</p>\n<p>A batch-trained model can make predictions in real time.</p>\n<p>Online learning specifically means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>the model itself updates incrementally from new data.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture defines batch learning as training on available data offline, while online learning updates sequentially or using mini-batches.</p>\n<h5>Trap: instance-based vs model-based</h5>\n<p>Instance:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which previous observations resemble this one?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Model:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What general relationship did I learn?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's almost exactly how the lecture summarizes them.</p>\n<h5>Trap: high training accuracy</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training accuracy = 99.9%\nTest accuracy = 60%\n</code></pre>\n<p>is <strong>not amazing</strong>.</p>\n<p>It screams:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Overfitting}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture defines overfitting as performing well on training data but failing to generalize.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d4\">PART B - PRACTICE SET 1</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. MCQ Practice - Set 1</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Try these <strong>without looking at the answers</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>Which best describes Machine Learning according to the engineering-oriented definition?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A computer follows manually programmed rules perfectly</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Performance on a task improves through experience</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A computer automatically becomes intelligent over time</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A computer stores increasing amounts of data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Mitchell's definition: performance $P$ on task $T$ improves through experience $E$.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H1</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Using Mitchell's definition (performance $P$ on task $T$ improves with experience $E$), which system is <strong>not</strong> an example of machine learning?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A spam filter whose precision rises after it is trained on more labelled emails.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A recommender whose click-through rate rises after it logs more user sessions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A sorting program that becomes faster after a programmer rewrites its algorithm by hand.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A game agent whose win rate rises after it plays more matches.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>All four improve, but only C improves through <strong>manual reprogramming</strong> rather than <strong>experience</strong>. A, B and D each have a task, a measured performance and more data (emails, sessions, matches) producing the improvement. Mitchell's definition is about where the improvement comes from, not whether it happens (Lecture 1, \"What is Machine Learning?\").</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A house-price model has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>historical house records</li>\n<li>task of predicting selling price</li>\n<li>mean prediction error</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What represents <strong>E</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicting selling price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Historical house records</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Mean prediction error</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The prediction algorithm</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Experience = historical data.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H2</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A hospital system studies 50,000 past chest X-rays that radiologists have labelled, in order to flag pneumonia on new scans. It is judged by the percentage of new scans it flags correctly. Which mapping of $T$, $P$ and $E$ is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> T = the 50,000 X-rays; P = flagging pneumonia; E = the percentage correct</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> T = flagging pneumonia; P = the number of X-rays processed; E = the radiologists' labels only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> T = labelling X-rays; P = the radiologists' accuracy; E = new scans</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> T = flagging pneumonia on scans; P = the percentage of new scans flagged correctly; E = the 50,000 labelled X-rays</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The <strong>task</strong> is what the system does, the <strong>performance measure</strong> is how it is scored, and the <strong>experience</strong> is the historical data it learns from. B uses a volume count instead of a quality measure. A rotates the three roles. C describes the radiologists, not the system.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A bank wants to predict whether a transaction is <code>\"Fraud\"</code> or <code>\"Legitimate\"</code>.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Fraud/Legitimate are categories.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H3</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which target turns the problem into <strong>classification</strong> rather than regression?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The number of fraudulent transactions a branch will see next month</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The RM amount a customer will lose to fraud</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A customer's exact credit score (300-850)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A credit-risk band (Low / Medium / High), even though the bands were originally cut from score ranges</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Classification predicts a <strong>category</strong>. Once scores are grouped into Low/Medium/High, the model's output is one of three labels, however they were derived. A (a count), B (a money amount) and C (an exact score) are all numeric targets, which makes them regression.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A property company predicts the selling price of a condominium in RM.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Anomaly detection</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>RM price is numerical.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H4</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which of these is a <strong>regression</strong> task?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicting whether a condominium will sell above RM500,000 (Yes/No)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predicting the number of days a condominium will stay on the market before it sells</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Predicting which of five price bands a condominium falls into</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Grouping condominiums by similarity when no prices are given</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Days on the market is a <strong>numerical quantity</strong>, so predicting it is regression. A turns price into a yes/no label (classification). C turns price into bands (classification). D has no target at all (clustering). The topic being \"price\" doesn't make a task regression - the type of the output does.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A retailer has customer transaction data but no predefined customer groups. An algorithm discovers four groups based on spending behaviour.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>No predefined labels; groups are discovered.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H5</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Every customer of a retailer already carries a \"VIP\" or \"Regular\" tag from its loyalty programme. A model is trained to assign new customers to one of these two tags. A colleague calls this clustering. Who is right?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The colleague: the model puts customers into groups, so it is clustering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Neither: it is regression, because spending is numeric.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Not the colleague: the groups are predefined labels the model learns to predict, so it is supervised classification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The colleague: humans created the tags, so the learning is unsupervised.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Clustering <strong>discovers</strong> groups that weren't given. Here the groups are <strong>known labels</strong> attached to the training examples, which is supervised classification. A confuses \"outputs a group\" with \"discovers groups\". B looks at the inputs instead of the target. D is backwards - human-provided labels are exactly what make learning supervised.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Which problem is most clearly anomaly detection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predict tomorrow's temperature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Identify suspicious transactions that differ from normal behaviour</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Divide customers into spending groups</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Predict whether a student will pass</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Anomalies are unusual patterns/events.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H6</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which scenario is best described as <strong>anomaly detection</strong> rather than classification?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Watching a turbine's vibration readings and flagging ones that deviate strongly from its normal operating pattern, when almost no failures have ever been recorded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training on 200,000 transactions each marked \"fraud\" or \"legitimate\" to label new transactions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Splitting turbines into groups with similar vibration profiles.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Predicting a turbine's remaining useful life in hours.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Anomaly detection looks for observations that <strong>differ from normal behaviour</strong>, which is useful when abnormal cases are rare or unlabelled. B has plenty of labelled examples of both classes, so it is classification even though the topic is fraud. C is clustering. D is regression.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Which is required for supervised learning?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No historical data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Labels/known target outcomes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rewards from an environment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Clusters must already exist</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Supervised learning uses labelled data.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H7</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which dataset can be used <strong>directly</strong> for supervised learning?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10,000 emails, none of which has been marked as spam or not spam</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 500 past loan applications, each recorded as \"repaid\" or \"defaulted\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A year of sensor logs containing only timestamps and readings, with no outcome recorded</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A game environment that returns points after each move</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Supervised learning needs <strong>inputs paired with known target outcomes</strong>. Only B has a label for every example. A and C are unlabelled (suitable for unsupervised methods). D provides <strong>rewards</strong> for actions, which is reinforcement learning, not labelled examples.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>An AI plays a game, receives +10 when it wins and -10 when it loses, and adjusts its behaviour.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Supervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unsupervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinforcement learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Batch learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Learning through reward/punishment.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H8</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A warehouse robot first watches 5,000 recorded packing sessions in which every frame is labelled with the correct arm movement, and learns to copy them. Later it packs boxes on its own, receiving +1 for each box delivered intact and -5 for each one dropped, and adjusts its strategy. Which learning types occur, in order?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsupervised, then supervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reinforcement, then supervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Supervised, then supervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Supervised, then reinforcement</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Phase 1 learns from examples labelled with the <strong>correct answer</strong>, so it is supervised. Phase 2 learns from <strong>rewards and penalties</strong> that follow its own actions, so it is reinforcement learning. C misses that no correct answers are given in phase 2, only a score. A and B get the order or the types wrong.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Which statement about unsupervised learning is most accurate according to the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It always predicts numerical values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It learns from labelled examples</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It finds patterns or similarities in unlabelled data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It requires reward signals</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Unsupervised learning looks for structure in unlabelled data.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H9</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which task could be done with <strong>unsupervised learning alone</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicting whether a student will pass, using last year's pass/fail records</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predicting house prices from past sales</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Learning to play a game from win/loss rewards</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Grouping news articles by similarity when no topics are given</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Unsupervised learning finds <strong>structure in unlabelled data</strong>, for example by grouping similar items. A and B need known outcomes (labels), so they are supervised. C learns from rewards, so it is reinforcement learning.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A credit model is retrained every month using all newly collected data. Between retrainings, the model does not update itself.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Online learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Batch learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinforcement learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Instance-based learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Retrain periodically rather than incrementally.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H10</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A credit model is retrained every Sunday on all the data collected so far. During the week it makes 50,000 predictions per hour but never changes its parameters. Which change would make it <strong>online learning</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Make each prediction faster.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Retrain every night instead of every Sunday.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Update the model's parameters incrementally as each newly labelled transaction arrives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Keep more historical data for the Sunday retrain.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Online learning means the model <strong>learns incrementally</strong> from new data as it arrives. B is the trap: retraining more often is still batch learning, only on a shorter schedule. A concerns prediction speed, not learning. D only enlarges the batch.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A model updates itself after receiving each new transaction.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Batch learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Online learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Model evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KDD</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Model updates incrementally.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H11</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For which situation is <strong>online learning</strong> the most natural choice?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A live price feed where new data arrives continuously and the patterns change quickly, so the model must keep adapting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A one-off study on a fixed, small dataset that will never change.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A dataset that has no labels at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A model that must be guaranteed never to overfit.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Online learning suits <strong>continuous data streams</strong> and <strong>changing patterns</strong>, because the model updates step by step without full retraining. B suits batch learning. C is about labels, which is a different axis (supervised vs unsupervised). D is false - online learning does not guarantee anything about overfitting.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A dataset is too large to fit into RAM. The algorithm loads part of the dataset, trains, then loads another part.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Out-of-core learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Instance-based learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>That's the lecture's definition of out-of-core learning.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H12</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A 2 TB dataset is used to train a model on a laptop with 16 GB of RAM. The training code streams the data in chunks and updates the model after each chunk. The model is then deployed and <strong>never updated again</strong>. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> This is online learning in production, because the model learns chunk by chunk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> This is instance-based learning, because the data is processed piece by piece.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> This is out-of-core learning: incremental updates are used because the data doesn't fit in memory, even though the deployed model doesn't keep learning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> This is impossible: training data must always fit in RAM.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The notes stress that out-of-core learning <strong>does not necessarily mean continuous learning in production</strong>. It uses incremental updates simply because the dataset is too large for memory. A confuses the training technique with production behaviour. B confuses chunking with comparing new cases to stored examples. D is exactly the limitation out-of-core learning removes.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A new customer is classified based largely on the five most similar previous customers.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Model-based learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Instance-based learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinforcement learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Batch learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Prediction relies on similar stored instances.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H13</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model predicts a customer's churn risk by finding the five most similar past customers. The stored customer base grows from 10,000 to 10 million. What is the most direct consequence?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The model becomes model-based, because there is now enough data to fit an equation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each prediction needs more work, because the new customer must be compared with far more stored examples.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Nothing changes, because instance-based models discard their training data after training.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model now needs reward signals.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Instance-based learning <strong>keeps the examples</strong> and predicts by <strong>similarity</strong> to them, so more stored examples means more comparisons per prediction. C is the reverse - it is model-based learning that summarises data into parameters. A and D confuse the learning type with how much data there is.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>A model learns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Price} = 300(\\text{Size})+50000$$</div>\n<p>and uses this relationship for new houses.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Instance-based learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Model-based learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unsupervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A general relationship/model has been learned.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H14</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model has learned $\\text{Price} = 300(\\text{Size}) + 50000$. What does it predict for a 1,200 sq ft house, and what kind of learning is this?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM360,000; model-based</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM410,000; instance-based</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM410,000; unsupervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM410,000; model-based</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$300 \\times 1200 + 50000 = 410{,}000$. The prediction comes from a <strong>learned general relationship</strong> (an equation), not from comparing with stored houses, so it is model-based. A forgets the intercept. B gets the number right but the learning type wrong. C ignores that the equation was fitted to known prices (labels).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Which result most strongly suggests <strong>overfitting</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Training accuracy 60%, test accuracy 61%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training accuracy 98%, test accuracy 65%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Training accuracy 90%, test accuracy 89%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Training accuracy 55%, test accuracy 50%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Huge training-test gap.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H15</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which result is the clearest case of <strong>overfitting</strong>, rather than underfitting or a mixed picture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Training 92%, test 90%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training 70%, test 52%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Training 75%, test 74%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Training 99%, test 81%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Overfitting means <strong>excellent training performance combined with much worse test performance</strong>. D has both. B also has an 18-point gap, but 70% on training data is itself weak, so it is not a clean overfitting case. A and C generalise consistently (C might be underfitting, A is healthy).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Which result most strongly suggests <strong>underfitting</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Training accuracy 99%, test accuracy 60%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training accuracy 94%, test accuracy 92%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Training accuracy 58%, test accuracy 56%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Training accuracy 100%, test accuracy 99%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Poor even on training data indicates insufficient learning.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H16</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A straight-line model is fitted to data that clearly follows a curve. It scores 61% on the training set and 60% on the test set. What is the best diagnosis and fix?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Overfitting; simplify the model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Good generalisation; deploy it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data leakage; re-split the data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Underfitting; use a more powerful model or better features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Weak performance on <strong>both</strong> training and test data, with no gap, means the model is too simple to capture the pattern. That is underfitting, fixed with a more powerful model, better features or fewer constraints. A applies the overfitting remedy, which would make it worse. B mistakes \"small gap\" for \"good\". C has no supporting evidence.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Which is a possible solution to overfitting according to the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Make the model more complicated</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Add more irrelevant features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Gather more training data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Increase noise in the data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>More training data is one lecture-listed remedy.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H17</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Select <strong>all</strong> that the lecture lists as ways to reduce <strong>overfitting</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Simplify the model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Gather more training data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Select a more powerful model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reduce noise in the training data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A, B, D</strong><p>The lecture lists three overfitting remedies: <strong>simplify the model, gather more training data, and reduce noise</strong>. C is an <strong>underfitting</strong> remedy - a more powerful model has even more capacity to memorise noise.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which can help fix underfitting?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Select a more powerful model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reduce the amount of useful data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Add more constraints to the model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove useful features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Underfitting can require a more powerful model.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H18</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which of these would <strong>not</strong> help a model that is <strong>underfitting</strong>?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Select a more powerful model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feed it better features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reduce the constraints on the model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Collect more rows of the same data while keeping the same simple model</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Underfitting means the model <strong>cannot capture the pattern</strong> even in data it has already seen. More data of the same kind doesn't add capacity, so the simple model stays too simple. A, B and C are the lecture's underfitting remedies: a more powerful model, better features, fewer constraints.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Why must training data be representative?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees zero error</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model must generalize to future observations similar to the target population</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It allows all algorithms to become supervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It removes the need for testing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Training data should represent the cases you want to generalize to.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H19</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A food-delivery demand model is trained only on weekday lunchtime orders from Kuala Lumpur. It is then used nationwide, 24 hours a day. What is the core problem?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Overfitting, because the dataset is too small</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Underfitting, because the model is too simple</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Nonrepresentative training data: the model never saw nights, weekends or other cities, so it must guess for them</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> There is no problem, provided the KL dataset is large</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Training data must <strong>cover the cases the model will face</strong>. The notes contrast insufficient data (a quantity problem) with nonrepresentative data (a coverage problem). This is a coverage problem, so more KL lunchtime data (D) wouldn't fix it. A and B are diagnosed from train/test errors, which aren't given here.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A dataset contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 220\nHeight = 8.7 metres\nIncome = missing\n</code></pre>\n<p>The major problem is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reinforcement learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Poor-quality/noisy data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Online learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Underfitting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Errors, missing data, outliers, noise.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H20</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which of these is <strong>not</strong> a data-quality problem in the lecture's sense?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An Age value of 220</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Income missing for 30% of customers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duplicated sensor readings with random noise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A straight-line model that is too simple for curved data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Errors (A), missing values (B), and noise or duplicates (C) are problems in the <strong>data</strong>. D is a problem with the <strong>model</strong> (underfitting): perfectly clean data would still be fitted badly. Telling data problems from model problems is the point of the \"main challenges\" part of the lecture.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Creating:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{DebtToIncome}=\\frac{\\text{Debt}}{\\text{Income}}$$</div>\n<p>from existing variables is an example of:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature engineering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinforcement learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Clustering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Creating a more useful feature.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H21</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A credit team does three things: (1) drops the random customer ID column, (2) computes DebtToIncome = Debt / Income, and (3) arranges to collect verified employment history, which it didn't have before. Using the lecture's three parts of feature engineering, which mapping is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) feature extraction, (2) feature selection, (3) creating new features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> (1) feature selection, (2) feature extraction, (3) creating new features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> (1) creating new features, (2) feature selection, (3) feature extraction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All three are data cleaning, not feature engineering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Selection</strong> keeps useful existing variables and drops useless ones, like the ID. <strong>Extraction</strong> combines existing information into something more useful, like the ratio. <strong>Creating new features</strong> means gathering information you don't yet have. A swaps the first two. D ignores that the lecture defines feature engineering as exactly these three activities.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Which CRISP-DM phase comes first?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business Understanding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data Preparation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>CRISP-DM begins with Business Understanding.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H22</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which sequence is the correct CRISP-DM order?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Business Understanding → Data Preparation → Data Understanding → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data Understanding → Business Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Deployment → Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Data Preparation → Modeling → Evaluation → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Business Understanding → Data Understanding → Modeling → Data Preparation → Evaluation → Deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>CRISP-DM starts from the <strong>business goal</strong>, then <strong>understands</strong> the data, then <strong>prepares</strong> it, then builds the model. The model is <strong>evaluated against the business goal</strong> before it is <strong>deployed</strong>. A prepares the data before understanding it. B starts with data and deploys before evaluating. D models before preparing the data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Which framework explicitly begins with <strong>Business Understanding</strong> and includes <strong>Deployment</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KDD</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SEMMA</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CRISP-DM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> K-Means</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>That's the major distinguishing feature of CRISP-DM here.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H23</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which pairing of each framework with its <strong>final</strong> step is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KDD → Deployment; SEMMA → Assess; CRISP-DM → Interpretation/Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KDD → Interpretation/Evaluation; SEMMA → Assess; CRISP-DM → Deployment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KDD → Assess; SEMMA → Deployment; CRISP-DM → Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All three end with Deployment.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>KDD ends by <strong>interpreting and evaluating</strong> the discovered patterns (knowledge). SEMMA ends with <strong>Assess</strong>. Only CRISP-DM goes on to <strong>Deployment</strong>. A swaps KDD and CRISP-DM. C mixes all three up. D is the trap the lecture warns about: only CRISP-DM names deployment.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Which correctly represents SEMMA?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sample → Explore → Modify → Model → Assess</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Select → Evaluate → Modify → Mine → Analyze</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Study → Explore → Model → Measure → Apply</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sample → Evaluate → Mine → Modify → Analyze</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>SEMMA = Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H24</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>In SEMMA, at which step would you create new variables, transform skewed ones and select the inputs for the model?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sample</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Explore</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Modify</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assess</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>SEMMA is <strong>Sample → Explore → Modify → Model → Assess</strong>. <strong>Modify</strong> is the transformation step: creating, transforming and selecting variables. Explore looks for patterns and anomalies without changing anything. Sample draws the subset. Assess judges the model.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Which is the correct broad idea behind KDD?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reward → action → reinforcement</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data → selection/preprocessing/transformation/mining/evaluation → knowledge</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Data → neural network only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Business objective → reinforcement → deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>KDD structures the journey from raw data to knowledge.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H25</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which sequence is the KDD process as presented in the lecture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Selection → Preprocessing → Transformation → Data Mining → Interpretation/Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Preprocessing → Selection → Data Mining → Transformation → Interpretation/Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Selection → Transformation → Preprocessing → Interpretation/Evaluation → Data Mining</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Business Understanding → Selection → Data Mining → Deployment</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>KDD first <strong>selects</strong> the relevant data, <strong>cleans</strong> it (preprocessing), <strong>transforms</strong> it into a useful form, <strong>mines</strong> it for patterns, and then <strong>interprets and evaluates</strong> them to produce knowledge. B and C shuffle the order. D mixes in CRISP-DM phases.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Answers - Set 1</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Mitchell's definition: performance $P$ on task $T$ improves through experience $E$.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Experience = historical data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Fraud/Legitimate are categories.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>RM price is numerical.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>No predefined labels; groups are discovered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Anomalies are unusual patterns/events.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Supervised learning uses labelled data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Learning through reward/punishment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Unsupervised learning looks for structure in unlabelled data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Retrain periodically rather than incrementally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Model updates incrementally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>That's the lecture's definition of out-of-core learning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Prediction relies on similar stored instances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>A general relationship/model has been learned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Huge training-test gap.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Poor even on training data indicates insufficient learning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>More training data is one lecture-listed remedy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td>Underfitting can require a more powerful model.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Training data should represent the cases you want to generalize to.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Errors, missing data, outliers, noise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>Creating a more useful feature.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>CRISP-DM begins with Business Understanding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>That's the major distinguishing feature of CRISP-DM here.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td>SEMMA = Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"><strong>B</strong></td>\n<td>KDD structures the journey from raw data to knowledge.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture describes overfitting solutions including simplifying the model, gathering more training data, and reducing noise; underfitting remedies include a more powerful model, better features, or fewer constraints.</p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d7\">PART C - CODING QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Coding Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These aren't copied from the slides-the lecture itself contains very little code-but they test whether you actually understand the lecture's concepts.</p>\n<h4>Coding Q1 - Classification or Regression?</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nX = [\n    [1000, 2],\n    [1500, 3],\n    [2000, 3],\n    [2500, 4]\n]\n\ny = [250000, 370000, 480000, 610000]\n\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X, y)\n\nprint(model.predict([[1800, 3]]))\n</code></pre>\n<p>What type of ML problem is this?</p>\n<p>A. Classification<br />\nB. Regression<br />\nC. Clustering<br />\nD. Reinforcement learning</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B - Regression</strong></p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p><code>y</code> contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">250000\n370000\n480000\n610000\n</code></pre>\n<p>These are continuous numerical targets.</p>\n<p>Don't be distracted by the algorithm name. Look at the <strong>target</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Coding Q2 - What does <code>.fit()</code> conceptually do?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.fit(X, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which description is best?</p>\n<p>A. Uses the model to predict unseen data<br />\nB. Deletes irrelevant observations<br />\nC. Learns a relationship between the input features and target<br />\nD. Calculates test accuracy automatically</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X,y\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Learning}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Model}$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.predict(new_X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>uses the learned relationship.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Coding Q3 - Spot the overfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you run:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">train_accuracy = model.score(X_train, y_train)\ntest_accuracy = model.score(X_test, y_test)\n\nprint(train_accuracy)\nprint(test_accuracy)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0.995\n0.621\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the most likely problem?</p>\n<p>A. Underfitting<br />\nB. Overfitting<br />\nC. Clustering<br />\nD. Out-of-core learning</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B - Overfitting</strong></p>\n<p>Interpret:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train}=99.5\\%$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Test}=62.1\\%$$</div>\n<p>The model learned the training data very well but fails to generalize.</p>\n<p>That's almost the textbook pattern from the lecture.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Coding Q4 - Instance-based or model-based?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Look at this simplified code:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=3)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nprediction = model.predict([new_customer])\n</code></pre>\n<p>The prediction is based on the nearest/similar stored observations.</p>\n<p>Which learning style best describes this according to Lecture 1?</p>\n<p>A. Model-based<br />\nB. Instance-based<br />\nC. Reinforcement<br />\nD. Batch-only</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B - Instance-based</strong></p>\n<p>The key isn't that <code>.fit()</code> appears.</p>\n<p>The important idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The system generalizes by comparing a new case against similar previous examples.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture describes instance-based learning precisely in those terms.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Coding Q5 - Batch vs online</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h5>System A</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.fit(all_training_data)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The company deploys it and retrains it every three months.</p>\n<h5>System B</h5>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">model.partial_fit(batch_1)\nmodel.partial_fit(batch_2)\nmodel.partial_fit(batch_3)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which is correct?</p>\n<p>A. A = online, B = batch<br />\nB. Both are batch<br />\nC. A = batch, B = online<br />\nD. Both are reinforcement</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: C</strong></p>\n<p><code>partial_fit()</code> here represents incremental updates.</p>\n<p>So conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A = \\text{Batch}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$B = \\text{Online}$$</div></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d13\">PART D - HARDER SCENARIOS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Harder MCQ Set - the kinds that actually catch people</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A spam classifier is trained once using one million labelled emails. It then immediately classifies every incoming email but never updates its parameters until next month's retraining.</p>\n<p>Which description is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Online supervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Batch supervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Online unsupervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is a good trap.</p>\n<p>It makes <strong>real-time predictions</strong>, but it does <strong>not learn online</strong>.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H26</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A fraud model updates its parameters immediately each time investigators confirm whether a new transaction was \"fraud\" or \"legitimate\". Which description is most accurate?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Online supervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Batch supervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Online unsupervised learning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>There are two separate questions. <strong>When does it learn?</strong> Incrementally, after each new example, so it is online. <strong>What does it learn from?</strong> Confirmed labels, so it is supervised. B gets the timing wrong. C ignores the labels. D would need rewards for the model's own actions, but here the feedback is the correct answer for each example.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A company gives an algorithm customer data with no customer segment labels. The algorithm discovers three clusters. Managers then name the groups <code>\"Budget\"</code>, <code>\"Regular\"</code>, and <code>\"Premium\"</code>.</p>\n<p>What learning happened?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Supervised classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unsupervised clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The humans naming clusters afterward does not magically make the original learning supervised.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H27</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>An algorithm finds three groups in unlabelled customer data, and managers name them \"Budget\", \"Regular\" and \"Premium\". A second model is then trained on these named customers to assign every <strong>new</strong> customer to one of the three groups. Which description is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both steps are supervised.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both steps are unsupervised.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Step 1 is unsupervised clustering; step 2 is supervised classification that uses the cluster names as labels.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Step 1 is classification; step 2 is clustering.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Step 1 <strong>discovers</strong> groups with no labels, so it is clustering. Naming the groups afterwards doesn't change that. Step 2 learns from those names as <strong>known labels</strong>, so it is supervised. A and B each miss one of the switches. D reverses them.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A model obtains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training error = 40%\nTest error = 42%\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which interpretation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Severe overfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Likely underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Excellent generalization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data leakage</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The training performance itself is poor.</p>\n<p>The model isn't even learning the known data adequately.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H28</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Model A has a training error of 40% and a test error of 42%. Model B has a training error of 2% and a test error of 41%. Their test errors are almost the same. Which conclusion is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both have the same problem, so they need the same fix.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A is underfitting and B is overfitting, so they need opposite fixes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A is overfitting and B is underfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both are fine, because their test errors match.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>You have to read the <strong>training</strong> error as well. A is poor even on training data, which is underfitting: it needs more power or better features. B fits training data almost perfectly but fails on new data, which is overfitting: it needs simplifying, more data or less noise. Similar test errors (A, D) can come from opposite causes. C reverses the diagnosis.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A model obtains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training error = 1%\nTest error = 24%\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which interpretation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Overfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Out-of-core learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Low training error + much higher test error = classic overfitting.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H29</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model has a training error of 1% and a test error of 24%. Which action is <strong>least</strong> likely to help?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Simplify the model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Gather more training data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Switch to an even more complex model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reduce noise in the training data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This is overfitting, and the lecture's remedies are A, B and D. A more complex model (C) has <strong>more</strong> capacity to memorise noise, which usually widens the gap between training and test error.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A hospital collects 2 million patient records, but every patient comes from the same specialist cardiac clinic. The model is deployed to predict outcomes for the entire population.</p>\n<p>What is the most obvious concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Dataset is too small</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training data may be nonrepresentative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Online learning is impossible</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Model must use reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>More data is not automatically better if the population is biased.</p>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes that training data should represent the new cases the model is expected to generalize to.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H30</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A tech website runs an online poll on smartphone preferences and gets 500,000 responses. The results are used to predict preferences across the <strong>whole country</strong>. Which statement is best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 500,000 responses guarantee the result represents the country.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The respondents (tech-site visitors who chose to answer) may not represent the country, and collecting more responses from the same source won't fix that.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The issue is underfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The poll should use reinforcement learning.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is a <strong>coverage</strong> problem (nonrepresentative data), not a quantity problem. A large sample drawn from a skewed source stays skewed - the same lesson as the cardiac-clinic example. A confuses size with representativeness. C and D are unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d15\">PART E - FRAMEWORKS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Framework questions are mostly about emphasis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_frameworks\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Highlighted bands show the steps the three frameworks share; CRISP-DM alone wraps them in business understanding and deployment.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Don't learn KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA as meaningless acronyms.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<h5>KDD</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Raw Data} \\rightarrow \\text{Knowledge}}$$</div>\n<p>Roughly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Selection\n→ Preprocessing\n→ Transformation\n→ Data Mining\n→ Interpretation/Evaluation\n</code></pre>\n<h5>CRISP-DM</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Business Problem} \\rightarrow \\text{Deployable Solution}}$$</div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Business Understanding\n→ Data Understanding\n→ Data Preparation\n→ Modeling\n→ Evaluation\n→ Deployment\n</code></pre>\n<h5>SEMMA</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Analytics/Model}-\\text{Building Process}}$$</div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sample\n→ Explore\n→ Modify\n→ Model\n→ Assess\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture identifies these three as the major data-mining process frameworks.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Framework MCQ traps</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>A team begins by asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What business objective are we trying to achieve?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Which framework most explicitly emphasizes this as its first phase?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SEMMA</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KDD</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CRISP-DM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Online learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H31</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A team is in the first CRISP-DM phase. Which output belongs to <strong>Business Understanding</strong> rather than Data Understanding?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A success criterion such as \"reduce monthly churn by 5 percentage points\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Summary statistics of the customer table</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A report on missing values and data quality</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A first exploratory chart of churn by region</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Business Understanding defines the <strong>objective and how success will be judged</strong>, before anyone looks at the data. B, C and D describe the data and belong to <strong>Data Understanding</strong>, the second phase.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Which stage belongs to SEMMA but not as a named CRISP-DM phase?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sample</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Modeling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Data Preparation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H32</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which statement correctly compares SEMMA and CRISP-DM?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SEMMA starts from a data sample and doesn't name business-understanding or deployment phases; CRISP-DM starts with Business Understanding and ends with Deployment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SEMMA begins with Business Understanding; CRISP-DM begins with Sample.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both begin with Sample and end with Deployment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> SEMMA has a Deployment phase; CRISP-DM does not.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>SEMMA focuses on the <strong>analytical workflow</strong> (Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess). CRISP-DM wraps modelling in <strong>business context</strong>: Business Understanding at the start, Deployment at the end. B and D reverse the frameworks. C invents phases.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>Which one ends with an explicit <strong>Deployment</strong> phase in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KDD</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SEMMA</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CRISP-DM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All three</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H33</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A churn model passes CRISP-DM <strong>Evaluation</strong>. Which statement best describes that phase and what comes next?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Evaluation only checks test accuracy; next comes Data Preparation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Evaluation checks the code for bugs; next comes Business Understanding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluation is the last phase of CRISP-DM.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluation checks whether the model actually meets the business objectives set at the start; if it does, Deployment follows.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>In CRISP-DM, Evaluation looks back at the <strong>business success criteria</strong>, not just accuracy, and is followed by <strong>Deployment</strong>. A narrows Evaluation to a metric and gets the next phase wrong. B is software testing. C forgets Deployment, the phase that makes CRISP-DM end-to-end.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l1p-d18\">PART F - FINAL REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. A final rapid-fire test</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Try answering these mentally in <strong>under 3 seconds each</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict stock price</td>\n<td>Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict stock goes Up/Down</td>\n<td>Classification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discover customer segments</td>\n<td>Clustering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect unusual credit-card purchase</td>\n<td>Anomaly detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn through reward</td>\n<td>Reinforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known labels</td>\n<td>Supervised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No labels</td>\n<td>Unsupervised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrain monthly</td>\n<td>Batch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Update every incoming observation</td>\n<td>Online</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data can't fit RAM</td>\n<td>Out-of-core</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare with nearest known cases</td>\n<td>Instance-based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn a general equation/tree/function</td>\n<td>Model-based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excellent train, poor test</td>\n<td>Overfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor train, poor test</td>\n<td>Underfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More complexity to fix?</td>\n<td>Usually underfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simpler model / more data may fix?</td>\n<td>Overfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data doesn't represent deployment population</td>\n<td>Nonrepresentative data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Create <code>BMI = weight / height²</code></td>\n<td>Feature engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business Understanding first</td>\n<td>CRISP-DM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample, Explore, Modify...</td>\n<td>SEMMA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raw data to knowledge</td>\n<td>KDD</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If you can answer that table instantly, you've got most of Lecture 1's MCQ territory covered.</p>\n<h4>The three distinctions I would drill hardest</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Classification vs Regression vs Clustering}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Batch vs Online}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Overfitting vs Underfitting}}$$</div>\n<p>Those are easiest to turn into deceptively worded scenarios. After that, know <strong>T-E-P</strong>, <strong>instance vs model-based</strong>, and the difference between <strong>KDD / CRISP-DM / SEMMA</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l1-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 1 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which completion of Tom Mitchell's definition is exactly right? \"A program learns from experience E with respect to task T and performance measure P if...\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ...its experience E grows each time it is evaluated with P on T.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ...its performance at tasks in T, as measured by P, improves with experience E.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ...it maximises P on the data in E, whatever the task.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ...the task T becomes easier as more experience E is stored.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Learning is defined by <strong>improvement of P on T caused by E</strong>. A only says data accumulates (storing data is not learning). C confuses fitting the training data with learning a task - maximising P on E alone is exactly what an overfitted model does. D says the task changes; in Mitchell's definition the task is fixed and the <em>system</em> gets better at it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>The lecture classifies ML along three independent questions. Which pairing of answers to question is <strong>wrong</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Supervised / Unsupervised / Reinforcement - does the machine receive correct answers while learning?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Batch / Online - how does new data arrive, i.e. when does the model learn?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Batch / Online - does the model generalise by comparing stored examples?</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Instance-based / Model-based - how does it generalise to new cases?</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>\"Comparing stored examples\" is the <strong>instance-based vs model-based</strong> dimension, not batch vs online. A, B and D are the lecture's three correct pairings. The trap is that both C and D talk about generalisation - but only D's options (instance vs model) answer it.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What does SEMMA stand for, in order?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Select, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sample, Evaluate, Modify, Model, Assess</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sample, Explore, Manipulate, Model, Apply</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess.</strong> A swaps in KDD's \"Selection\"; B swaps in CRISP-DM's \"Evaluation\"; D changes two words. Each distractor borrows a real step name from a neighbouring framework, which is exactly how exam distractors are built.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>A spam filter classifies every incoming email within milliseconds, but its parameters only change when the team retrains it every Sunday. Why is it <strong>not</strong> online learning?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because online learning means the model is hosted on the internet, and this one runs on a local server.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because \"online\" describes how the model learns - incremental parameter updates as data arrives - not how fast it predicts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because spam filtering is a classification task, and online learning only applies to regression.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because weekly retraining is too frequent to count as batch learning, so it is a third category.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Batch vs online is about <strong>when learning happens</strong>. Fast real-time <em>prediction</em> says nothing about learning; this model learns in weekly batch events, so it is batch learning. A is the classic \"online = internet\" confusion. C is false - online learning works for any supervised task. D invents a category; periodic retraining is the textbook batch pattern.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>As you increase a model's complexity, its training error keeps falling. What does that fact <strong>on its own</strong> tell you about generalisation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - training error falls with flexibility even after test error has started to rise, so you need unseen data to judge generalisation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model is generalising better, because lower training error always means lower test error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model is overfitting, because falling training error is the definition of overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model is underfitting, because it has not yet reached zero training error.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture's complexity graph shows training error decreasing monotonically while <strong>test error is U-shaped</strong>. So falling training error is compatible with both improving and worsening generalisation. B ignores the right half of the U. C is wrong - overfitting is a <em>gap</em> between training and test performance, not falling training error. D wrongly treats zero training error as the goal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>A hospital trains on 2 million records - all from one specialist cardiac clinic - and plans to deploy nationwide. Why would collecting another 2 million records <strong>from the same clinic</strong> not fix the main problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because doubling the data always causes underfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the extra data would make the model overfit to cardiac patients.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because large datasets require online learning, which this hospital does not use.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the problem is nonrepresentative data: more of the same population adds no information about the patients the model will actually see.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The lecture separates <strong>quantity</strong> from <strong>representativeness</strong>. The model will generalise to a population it has never sampled; more cardiac-clinic data cannot represent rural GPs or children. A and C are false generalisations. B is tempting, but the issue is not memorising noise - it is that the training distribution differs from the deployment distribution.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A k-NN classifier predicts whether a student passes. The four nearest stored students to a new student are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distance</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1.0</td>\n<td>Fail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.2</td>\n<td>Pass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.3</td>\n<td>Pass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3.0</td>\n<td>Fail</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What does it predict with <strong>k = 1</strong> and with <strong>k = 3</strong> (simple majority vote)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fail with k = 1, Pass with k = 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pass with k = 1, Pass with k = 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fail with k = 1, Fail with k = 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pass with k = 1, Fail with k = 3</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>k = 1 uses only the closest example (distance 1.0, <strong>Fail</strong>). k = 3 uses the three closest - Fail, Pass, Pass - and the majority is <strong>Pass</strong>. The flip shows why k matters: a small k follows individual (possibly noisy) neighbours, a larger k smooths the decision. The fourth neighbour (3.0) is never used in either case.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A bank has 1,000,000 transactions, of which 0.2% are fraudulent. A model labels <strong>every</strong> transaction as legitimate. What are its accuracy and the number of frauds it catches?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.2% accuracy, 2,000 frauds caught</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 99.8% accuracy, 0 frauds caught</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 99.8% accuracy, 2,000 frauds caught</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 50% accuracy, 1,000 frauds caught</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>0.2% of 1,000,000 = 2,000 frauds. Predicting \"legitimate\" for everything is right for the 998,000 legitimate ones → <strong>99.8% accuracy</strong>, and catches <strong>0</strong> frauds. This is why the lecture insists evaluation must connect back to the business objective: accuracy alone rewards a useless model on imbalanced data.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A model-based learner fitted $\\text{Price} = 250 \\times \\text{Size} + 50{,}000$ on houses between 800 and 2,500 sq ft. A client asks for a 20,000 sq ft estate. What does the model output, and what is the main concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM550,000 - the model caps predictions at its training range.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM5,050,000, and it is reliable because model-based learners generalise to any input.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM5,050,000, but this is extrapolation far beyond the training range, so the learned relationship may not hold.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An instance-based learner would return exactly RM5,050,000 as well, so there is no concern.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>250 x 20,000 + 50,000 = <strong>5,050,000</strong>. A linear model will happily extrapolate, but nothing in the data shows the relationship stays linear at 8x the largest training house - that is a <strong>representativeness</strong> problem in the input space. A invents a cap. B overstates what \"generalisation\" means. D is false: a k-NN model would return values near its largest stored houses, not an extrapolated number.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Three models are evaluated on the same split:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Train accuracy</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Test accuracy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">72%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">70%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">99%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">71%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">88%</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">86%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which reading is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B is best because it has the highest training accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A overfits, B underfits, C is in between.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All three generalise equally, because their test accuracies are within 16 points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A underfits, B overfits, and C generalises best.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A is poor on <strong>both</strong> sets (too simple → underfitting). B has a 28-point <strong>gap</strong> between train and test (memorising → overfitting). C is high on both with a small gap - the sweet spot. A is the \"lowest training error wins\" trap the lecture warns about; B reverses the definitions; C ignores both the levels and the gaps.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A recommendation model was trained once on January data. Without any code changes, its accuracy falls a little every month until June. What is the most likely explanation, and which ML dimension does it point to?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The data the model sees has drifted away from January's; a batch model never updates, so it needs periodic retraining or online learning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model is overfitting more every month, so it needs fewer features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> More data arrived each month, which made the model underfit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model is instance-based and its stored examples are being deleted.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The model is unchanged; the <strong>world</strong> changed - that is drift. The lecture's batch-vs-online dimension exists for exactly this: batch models stay frozen until retrained, while online learners can adapt. B: the model's complexity hasn't changed, so its overfitting can't grow. C: the model never sees the new data, so it can't affect fitting. D is invented.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A retailer runs k-means to find customer groups. Managers name the clusters \"Budget\", \"Regular\" and \"Premium\", then train a classifier on those names to tag new customers. Which description is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The whole pipeline is supervised, because names were added.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The whole pipeline is unsupervised, because the labels came from clustering.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Stage 1 is unsupervised; stage 2 is supervised learning on labels that stage 1 produced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Stage 2 is reinforcement learning, because managers give feedback.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Naming clusters afterwards <strong>does not</strong> make the clustering supervised (the lecture's explicit trap), so A is wrong for stage 1. But once those names are used as targets $y$, stage 2 <em>is</em> supervised - so B is wrong for stage 2. D confuses one-off labelling with a reward signal from an environment.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A team reports 99.9% accuracy for a card-fraud model and asks to deploy. Which response best reflects CRISP-DM's Evaluation phase?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Approve deployment - 99.9% is excellent for any model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Go back to Data Preparation, because accuracy above 99% always means leakage.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Deploy now and evaluate later from customer complaints.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ask whether it meets the business objective - frauds caught vs legitimate transactions blocked - before deciding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>CRISP-DM's Evaluation phase judges the model <strong>against the business success criteria</strong> defined in Business Understanding, not against a generic metric. With fraud at ~0.1%, 99.9% accuracy can come from catching nothing. B overreacts: high accuracy can be legitimate, but it must be <em>checked</em>. A and C skip evaluation entirely.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A phone app must predict on-device in under 10 ms. The training set has 50 million examples. Which judgement is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Instance-based (k-NN), because it needs no training and is therefore fastest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Model-based, because predictions use a compact set of learned parameters instead of searching 50 million stored examples.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Either is equally suitable, because both generalise from examples.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Instance-based, because it is always more accurate than model-based learning.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Instance-based learning moves the work to <strong>prediction time</strong>: it must store and search the examples. On a phone with tight memory and a 10 ms budget, 50M examples is impractical. A model-based learner compresses the data into parameters. A confuses \"no training\" with \"fast prediction\". C ignores the deployment constraint. D is a false absolute.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A research group mines five years of student-portal logs to discover interesting behaviour patterns. There is no business client and nothing will be deployed. Which framework's emphasis fits most naturally?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KDD, because its whole purpose is turning raw data into knowledge, ending in interpretation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CRISP-DM, because only CRISP-DM can be used for academic data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SEMMA, because it is the only framework with an exploration step.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> None - frameworks only apply to commercial projects.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>KDD's endpoint is <strong>knowledge</strong> after interpretation/evaluation, which matches a discovery-focused study. CRISP-DM <em>could</em> be used, but its distinguishing strengths - business understanding and deployment - are exactly what this project lacks, so B's \"only\" is wrong. C is false: KDD has selection/preprocessing and CRISP-DM has data understanding. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design the learning setup for a news app. Users' interests shift daily, the click history is far too large for memory, and every impression is logged as clicked / not clicked. Which design fits the lecture's concepts best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsupervised clustering retrained once a year on a sample.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Instance-based k-NN that keeps every click in RAM.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reinforcement learning only, with no use of the logged clicks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Supervised learning (clicks are labels), trained online / out-of-core in mini-batches, with performance monitored over time.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Clicks are labels → <strong>supervised</strong>. Interests shift daily → <strong>online</strong> updates. The history doesn't fit in memory → <strong>out-of-core</strong> mini-batches. A ignores both the labels and the drift. B fails on memory. C throws away a large labelled signal that already exists.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You must design an experiment that shows convincingly whether your model overfits. Which plan is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Compare the training accuracy of several models; the highest is the least overfit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Hold out data the model never trains on, then plot training and held-out error as complexity (or training size) increases, and look for a widening gap.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluate the model twice on the training data to check that the score is stable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Add more features until training accuracy reaches 100%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Overfitting is defined as performing well on training data but <strong>not generalising</strong>, so it can only be shown with unseen data. Plotting both curves against complexity reproduces the lecture's key graph and shows where the gap opens. A and C only use training data; D actively <em>causes</em> overfitting.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Your manager says: \"Use ML to reduce churn.\" Which plan best follows CRISP-DM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Define churn and a measurable target (e.g. cut churn by 2 points at an acceptable offer cost) → check what data exists and its quality → prepare features → model → evaluate against the target → deploy with monitoring, looping back where needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pick the most accurate algorithm from a benchmark → train on all data → deploy → define what churn means afterwards.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sample → Explore → Modify → Model → Assess, and stop there.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Collect as much data as possible first, then decide which business problem it can solve.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>CRISP-DM <strong>starts</strong> with Business Understanding (what churn is, what success means), then Data Understanding, Preparation, Modeling, Evaluation against the business target, and Deployment - and it is iterative. B inverts the order. C is SEMMA, which has no business-understanding or deployment phase. D lets the data choose the problem.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A language model is pre-trained by predicting masked words in billions of unlabelled sentences, then fine-tuned on 1,000 labelled reviews for sentiment. How is this best classified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reinforcement learning, because the model is rewarded for correct words.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Self-supervised pre-training (labels created from the data itself) followed by supervised fine-tuning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unsupervised clustering followed by classification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Semi-supervised learning in the sense the lecture slide uses for reinforcement learning.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Masking creates labels from the raw text itself (<strong>self-supervised</strong>); the 1,000 labelled reviews make the second stage <strong>supervised</strong>. A: there is no environment or delayed reward. C: no clusters are formed. D: the lecture's slide calls RL \"semi-supervised\", but standard terminology separates RL from semi-supervised learning - and neither describes masked-word pre-training.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>For squared error, expected test error decomposes as $\\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\text{Irreducible noise}$. A model has bias 0.3, variance 0.04 and noise variance 0.01. What is its expected squared error?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.35</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.05</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.14</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$0.3^2 + 0.04 + 0.01 = 0.09 + 0.04 + 0.01 = 0.14$. A adds the bias without squaring it. B drops the bias entirely. C forgets the noise term. Underfitting in the lecture's sense is <strong>high bias</strong>; overfitting is <strong>high variance</strong> - this decomposition is the formal version of the complexity curve.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>What is the largest number of points in general position in 2D that a linear classifier (a line with a bias term) can <strong>shatter</strong> - i.e. separate correctly under every possible labelling?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Unlimited</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The VC dimension of linear classifiers in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is $d+1$, so in 2D it is <strong>3</strong>. Any 3 non-collinear points can be split in all $2^3$ ways. For 4 points, the XOR labelling (opposite corners share a label) cannot be separated by any line. This is the formal measure of the \"model capacity\" that drives the overfitting/underfitting trade-off.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Of 10,000 transactions, 100 are fraud. A model flags 200 transactions; 80 of them are truly fraud. What are its precision and recall?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Precision 0.40, recall 0.80</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Precision 0.80, recall 0.40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Precision 0.80, recall 0.80</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Precision 0.40, recall 0.008</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Precision = true flagged / all flagged = 80 / 200 = <strong>0.40</strong>. Recall = true flagged / all frauds = 80 / 100 = <strong>0.80</strong>. B swaps the two. D divides by all 10,000 transactions. Precision and recall are how a fraud team turns \"don't trust 99.9% accuracy\" into numbers that match the business cost of missed frauds vs blocked customers.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Data are uniform in a $d$-dimensional unit cube. To capture 10% of the data, a cube-shaped neighbourhood needs edge length $0.1^{1/d}$. In $d = 10$ that is about:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.32</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.79</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.98</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$0.1^{1/10} \\approx 0.794$. The neighbourhood must span <strong>79%</strong> of each axis to hold 10% of the points. So \"nearest\" neighbours are no longer local, which undermines instance-based methods like k-NN - the <strong>curse of dimensionality</strong>. B is the 2D answer ($\\sqrt{0.1}$). D is roughly the $d = 100$ answer.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A deployed fraud model's <strong>input</strong> distributions are stable (population-stability index ≈ 0 on every feature). Yet as delayed labels arrive, its precision falls from 0.60 to 0.35. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Covariate shift - the features have drifted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Concept drift - the relationship $P(y \\mid x)$ has changed (fraudsters changed tactics), so the model must be retrained on recent labelled data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Underfitting - the model was too simple from the start.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A data-quality bug, because performance cannot change while inputs are stable.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>If $P(x)$ is unchanged but performance degrades, the mapping from $x$ to $y$ has changed: <strong>concept drift</strong>. Fraud is the lecture's own example of a changing environment, so online learning or frequent retraining is needed. A is ruled out by the stable PSI. C cannot explain a <em>decline</em> over time. D is false: performance changes whenever $P(y \\mid x)$ changes.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>In a sales-forecasting hackathon, your random 5-fold cross-validation score is 0.95, but the private leaderboard (which uses future months) gives 0.70. What is the most likely cause?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The model underfits; add more trees.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The leaderboard is wrong, because cross-validation is always reliable.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Random folds mix future rows into training, so the validation is easier than forecasting the real future; use time-ordered splits.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Too few folds; using 10 folds would close the gap.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With time-dependent data, random folds let the model learn from rows that come <em>after</em> the rows it is validated on. That leaks future information - the same principle as the lecture's leakage warning, applied to the <strong>split</strong>. Time-based splits (train on the past, validate on the future) estimate real deployment. D keeps the same leak. A and B misread the evidence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>This streaming code fails on the very first batch:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.linear_model import SGDClassifier\nclf = SGDClassifier()\nfor X_batch, y_batch in stream:          # first batch contains only class 0\n    clf.partial_fit(X_batch, y_batch)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why, and what is the fix?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>partial_fit</code> doesn't exist; use <code>fit</code> in the loop.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Online learning needs the whole dataset in memory; load it first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> On the first call the model must be told every possible class: <code>clf.partial_fit(X_batch, y_batch, classes=[0, 1])</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The batch is too small; batches need at least 1,000 rows.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>In incremental (online) learning, a batch may not contain every label. scikit-learn therefore requires <code>classes=</code> on the <strong>first</strong> <code>partial_fit</code> call; without it, it raises an error. A loses the online behaviour - each <code>fit</code> would restart from scratch. B contradicts the point of online / out-of-core learning. D is invented.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>In very over-parameterised models (e.g. large neural networks), test error can rise near the point where parameters ≈ training samples, then <strong>fall again</strong> as the model grows further (\"double descent\"). How does this relate to the lecture's U-shaped complexity curve?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It proves overfitting does not exist, so the U-curve should be discarded.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The U-curve describes the classical regime; beyond the interpolation threshold, implicit regularisation can make larger models generalise better, so complexity alone doesn't determine overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Double descent only happens with k-NN, so the U-curve still holds for every parametric model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Double descent means training error rises again for very large models.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Double descent <strong>extends</strong> the classical picture rather than refuting it. The U-curve holds up to the interpolation threshold. Past it, among the many models that fit the training data perfectly, the optimiser tends to find \"simple\" (low-norm) ones, so test error can fall again. A overclaims. C is false - the effect is best known in linear models and neural networks. D is false - training error stays near zero in the over-parameterised regime.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Learning curves for a churn model show training and validation accuracy converging at about 0.68 as the training set grows from 5k to 500k rows. What is the best next investment?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Collect another 500k rows - more data always helps.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Add regularisation to reduce variance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use a simpler model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Increase model capacity or engineer better features, because the curves show high bias.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Converged, <strong>low</strong> curves with no gap mean the model can't represent the pattern (underfitting / high bias). The lecture's remedies are a more powerful model and better features. A and B treat the wrong problem: more data and regularisation fix <em>variance</em> (a gap between the curves), which isn't present. C makes the bias worse.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A team tunes 200 hyperparameter combinations with 5-fold cross-validation and reports the <strong>best</strong> cross-validation score as the model's expected performance. What is wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - cross-validation scores are unbiased.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The reported score is optimistically biased, because the folds were used to choose the hyperparameters; estimate performance with nested cross-validation or an untouched test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5 folds is too few; with 10 folds the best score would be unbiased.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hyperparameter tuning causes underfitting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Picking the best of 200 configurations on the same folds <strong>fits the validation data</strong> - a selection effect, the evaluation-level version of overfitting. An honest estimate needs data that played no part in any choice: an outer loop (nested CV) or a held-out test set. C keeps the same selection bias. D is unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>CRISP-DM ends at Deployment, but a deployed fraud model can quietly decay. Which monitoring design best closes the loop?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Re-run the original test set every month; if the score is unchanged, nothing has changed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Monitor input drift and the prediction distribution, track precision/recall as delayed labels arrive, alert when they cross thresholds, and retrain from versioned data/model snapshots, feeding back into Business and Data Understanding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Retrain from scratch every day regardless of performance, with no versioning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only log errors that customers complain about.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Monitoring must catch <strong>covariate drift</strong> (inputs), output shift, and <strong>concept drift</strong> (label-based metrics), and versioning makes retraining reproducible - the operational version of CRISP-DM's loop back to the start. A: a fixed old test set cannot reveal drift. C wastes effort and gives no idea whether a change helped. D only sees failures customers happen to notice.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
